Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of kernel I/O paths, concurrency, and memory barriers.

Key Concepts

- Lock-free ring buffers and CAS
- Per-slot versioning to prevent double-consume
- Memory order fences between ISR, producer, and consumer
- Wakeup semantics and fairness
- Testing under bursty I/O and backpressure

Code Example

```c
// Minimal producer (ISR) enqueue
int enq(struct ring *r, struct evt *e){
  int idx = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed) & (RING_MASK);
  if (atomic_load_explicit(&r->slots[idx].claimed, memory_order_acquire))
    return -1;
  r->slots[idx].evt = *e;
  atomic_store_explicit(&r->slots[idx].claimed, 1, memory_order_release);
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->head, 1, memory_order_release);
  return 0;
}
```

