Why This Is Asked
Tests lock-free data structures, cross-core coordination, and end-to-end IO flow. It gauges ability to design scalable, low-latency paths with correct completion routing.

Key Concepts
- Per-device queues and shared completion path
- Lock-free enqueue/dequeue via CAS
- Correct delivery to originating process
- Backpressure, burst handling, and deadlock avoidance

Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-C: per-device ring enqueue
typedef struct req { void* buf; size_t len; int op; } req_t;
typedef struct ring { _Atomic(int) head, tail; req_t* buf[N]; } ring_t;
int enqueue(ring_t* r, req_t* e){
  int t = atomic_load(&r->tail);
  int next = (t + 1) % N;
  if (next == atomic_load(&r->head)) return -1; // full
  r->buf[t] = e;
  atomic_store(&r->tail, next);
  return 0;
}
req_t* dequeue(ring_t* r){
  int h = atomic_load(&r->head);
  int t = atomic_load(&r->tail);
  if (h == t) return NULL; // empty
  req_t* e = r->buf[h];
  atomic_store(&r->head, (h + 1) % N);
  return e;
}
``` 

Follow-up Questions
- How to scale with multiple devices sharing completion rings?
- How to handle out-of-order completions and per-process cancellation?
- What tests validate fairness under mixed device load?

## Why This Is Asked
Exposes real-world lock-free design pitfalls: memory ordering, ABA, and visibility in a high-throughput ring buffer.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free data structures and CAS
- Per-slot sequencing to avoid ABA
- Memory ordering on x86_64 vs ARM64
- Correctness under contention and wrap-around

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal enqueue (producer)
bool enqueue(slot_t* buf, int n, uint64_t key, uint64_t val) {
  static thread_local uint64_t prod = 0; // per-producer sequence
  uint64_t idx = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1) % n;
  slot_t* s = &buf[idx];
  s->key = key;
  s->val = val;
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, prod*2+1, memory_order_release);
  prod++;
  return true;
}

// Minimal dequeue (consumer)
bool dequeue(slot_t* buf, int n, uint64_t* out_key, uint64_t* out_val) {
  static uint64_t tail = 0;
  slot_t* s = &buf[tail % n];
  uint64_t seq = atomic_load_acquire(&s->seq);
  if ((seq & 1) == 0) return false; // empty
  *out_key = s->key;
  *out_val = s->val;
  atomic_store_explicit(&s->seq, seq+1, memory_order_release);
  tail++;
  return true;
}
```

